  {
    "episode_id": 89378,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89378.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "That would be, as a matter of fact, there's members of Congress that have been in trouble for that and went to jail. We've had senators lose their Senate seats because of the misuse of campaign plans. So those are criminal complaints where everything is done above board, where you have to have a grand jury involved. But when you use counterintelligence, and this is the key, you do not have a grand jury. You only have a secret court. But that's only if you go to get a warrant. Before all that, you can use counterintelligence to investigate a campaign. Now, keep in mind, we oversee counterintelligence at the House Intelligence Committee. Now, I never even knew that they could look at a campaign. I never even thought that they would even think about it about using these special capabilities. Because counterintelligence is really used to track down terrorists, track down spies, track down people that are trying to steal our nation's secrets. That's what counterintelligence is supposed to be used for. Never in a million years did I ever think that they'd use the secret, very powerful capabilities to not only target a political campaign, but target a presidential campaign, which is even worse. And then we can go on from there, but everybody knows that this investigation was largely predicated on the Clinton paid for dirt that was in the steel dossier.",
