    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "Well, that's why I said earlier, I was alluding to it, that this was really not a collusion investigation, collusion conspiracy investigation. It was actually an obstruction of justice trap that was set. So they didn't have any evidence of collusion or conspiracy at the time that Mueller walked in the door. Now, how do I know that? because we were already running an investigation at the House Intelligence Committee. We had supposedly received every bit of information that had anything to do with any Russian contacting anyone in the Trump campaign. Okay, so we knew at that point, you know, look, where's the collusion at? Where's the conspiracy? Who do you have? There was no one. There was no one except for the people that were mentioned in the Steele dossier. There was all rumors in any new window. So we had no hard evidence from our 17 intelligence agencies. We had no, I shouldn't even say hard evidence. We had zero evidence that there was any evidence of collusion. So remember, Peter Strach says in a text message, just two weeks before the special counsel is appointed, that there's no there there. Meaning that there was no evidence of collusion or conspiracy with the Russians on behalf of the Trump campaign. So then you move forward from there and they say, well, Trump fired Comey. Well, Comey leaked his memos illegally to the New York Times. But even in the memos, Trey Gowdy always liked to say that exhibit number one, that Trump wasn't trying to obstruct justice were the Comey memos. Because Trump in the memos is saying, I want you to investigate this. This isn't true. These things didn't happen. He was very upset about the P tape. I won't get into the P tape",
